# The same work, run at a rate. A workload line is the whole of what makes a test
# workload-bearing (`D99`) — these are the functional tests above with a rate wrapped round them,
# which is `D1043`'s point: LOAD is a kind of work, not a kind of test.
#
# Every workload-bearing test must carry a threshold (`TF033`) — a workload test's verdict comes
# only from its thresholds, so one with none generates real load, reports real numbers and passes
# unconditionally. A `duration` threshold must be paired with an unscoped `error rate` one, because
# a duration threshold reads only the iterations that succeeded and cannot see failure on its own.

@catalogue @workload
test "the catalogue holds up when everyone arrives at once"
  ramp to 4 users over 2s
  threshold p95 duration is less than 250ms
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  api GET /items
  expect status equals 200

# The one test that is workload-bearing AND judged by a scan, which is the combination worth
# showing: a security rule reads the response it is given, so under load it reads the responses
# load produces — including the error path, which is where a 503 page with no CSP or a stack frame
# under stress would appear. Nothing else in this example can catch that.
#
# **The workload is bounded on purpose, and `M205-07` is why.** A scan rule is judged once per response
# and every judgement is recorded, so the report holds one row per response that violates — which is
# correct, and is what this test is for. It is also multiplicative: `ramp to 3 users over 2s` against
# an endpoint this fast issued ~29,000 requests, so following this example's own instruction (delete
# `HttpOnly;` in `server.mjs`) wrote **29,381 findings — one weakness, stated 29,381 times — a 55 MB
# `report.html`, a 29.9 MB SARIF and a page 3.5 million pixels tall.** Measured 2026-09-19.
#
# So the workload is a fixed count, the shape the third test below already uses and explains: 20
# requests, so the same instruction now writes 20 rows. The duplication is accepted rather than
# collapsed — the scan reports everything it catches for this workload, which is the honest thing
# for it to do — and the test simply does not ask it to catch the same thing thirty thousand times.
#
# **A rate was tried first and is the wrong tool here, for a reason worth keeping.** `hold 5 rps for
# 4s` is also ~20 requests, but it bounds by *stretching the run*, and this suite's headline verdict
# is a property of the whole run's CPU: the rate-capped version dropped the run from `cpu 108%` to
# under the bottleneck threshold and flipped it from INCONCLUSIVE to PASS — silently falsifying
# README's first teaching point. A count finishes fast, the catalogue ramp above still saturates the
# generator, and the verdict is unchanged (measured: INCONCLUSIVE, cpu 112%). **Bounding one test's
# workload can change another test's verdict**, because the bottleneck judgement is the run's, not
# the test's.
@identity @workload
test "signing in holds up, and is still judged while it does"
  run 20 iterations across 2 users
  threshold p95 duration is less than 400ms
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  api POST /login body { email: "sam@example.com", password: "hunter2" }
  expect response has no serious security violations

# No request at all. This measures tflw's OWN generator: `unique` mints a value per iteration, and
# a run whose bottleneck is the generator rather than the target reports numbers about itself.
# It is the one shape that reaches the LOAD door alone — nothing here is api, browser or scan
# evidence, because `let` and `log` are evidence of nothing (`STEP_LENS`).
#
# **A count, not a rate, and the first draft got this wrong.** `ramp to 20 users over 3s` against a
# body that does no I/O ran 1,053,717 iterations in three seconds and tflw correctly declared
# *itself* the bottleneck — which marks every threshold in the run `skipped` and the whole run
# INCONCLUSIVE. A rate is the right shape for work that waits on something; for work that does not,
# a fixed count finishes and can be judged.
@references @workload
test "minting order references keeps up with the checkout rate"
  run 50 iterations across 2 users
  threshold p95 duration is less than 50ms
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  let ref = unique like "ORD-######"
  log "minted {ref}"

# The one statement in the language that spends time on purpose. `pause` is **per-iteration
# pacing** and the checker holds it to that (`TF033`): outside a workload-bearing test there is
# nothing for it to pace, and waiting for something to *become* true is `wait until`. Here it is a
# reader who looks at the shelf, thinks, and looks again — which is what a shop's traffic is, and
# is a different load profile from the same twenty requests issued flat out.
#
# The threshold is loose for the obvious reason: the pause is inside the iteration, so it is inside
# the duration the threshold reads. A p95 tighter than the pause would be a test of arithmetic.
#
# **IT IS `@pacing` AND NOT `@workload`, AND THE REASON IS THIS FILE'S OWN WARNING COMING TRUE.**
# The note above says *bounding one test's workload can change another test's verdict, because the
# bottleneck judgement is the run's and not the test's* — and this test is what demonstrated it.
# Measured, same box, same tree, one tag apart:
#
#     --tag workload, without it : INCONCLUSIVE 3/3
#     --tag workload, with it    : PASS 4/4 · generator cpu 74%
#
# A pause is idle time inside the run, so it dilutes the CPU rate the bottleneck judgement reads,
# and the example's first teaching point — that a load verdict against a toy on loopback is not
# available at any setting — would have quietly become false. So it runs on its own:
# `npm run example:pacing`.
@pacing
test "a reader who pauses between pages is a different kind of load"
  run 10 iterations across 2 users
  threshold p95 duration is less than 800ms
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
  api GET /items
  expect status equals 200
  pause 200ms
